fixup! Bug 2061218 - Save calendar event edits in new calendar edit dialog. r=#thunderbird-front-end-reviewers

Failure causes:
test_storeUpdatesBeforeConnection leaves its replacement dialog open after the task returns. registerCleanupFunction runs when the browser-test file finishes, so the temporary dialog remains during test_closeButtonDiscardsEditedDraft. Both dialogs share their position and stacking level. The replacement can intercept the Close click while the following task waits for the original dialog's close event. Cached job 601765223 confirms that the lifecycle assertions pass and the Close task then times out. Click interception is supported by source inspection; no new native reproduction completed.

This explains the supplied patch classifications for jobs 601765223, 601765152, 601765159, 601765183, 601765184, 601765195, 601765199, 601765200, 601765201, 601765231, 601765267, 601765310, 601765335, 601765354, and 601765364. The supplied unrelated classifications remain unchanged for jobs 601765216, 601765297, 601765143, 601765165, 601765204, 601765214, 601765230, 601765246, 601765247, 601765260, and 601765262. No unknown classification was changed.

The cumulative fixup also retains the earlier routing repair. That repair removes privileged MouseEvent("click") construction, which triggered Gecko's debug assertion requiring PointerEvent.

Changes:
The full cumulative fixup changes only mail/components/calendar/test/browser/browser_calendarEventEditSave.js: 29 added lines and seven removed lines relative to the owning Save commit.

The new cleanup change names the existing callback cleanupDialogs and keeps its early registration as fallback cleanup. After the lifecycle assertions, the task establishes a wait for the replacement's open attribute to clear, closes the replacement, awaits that state, and calls cleanupDialogs before returning. Cleanup closes both temporary dialogs, disposes both store subscriptions, and removes the dialogs and container. A new assertion checks that the fixture has no open dialog. A comment explains why cleanup must happen before the next task. Source inspection confirms that Redux unsubscribe safely ignores repeated calls, so later fallback cleanup can run again.

Preserved the routing repair already committed in abb035fd8d657c7d526495095e466977f6378500, which reused the routing block from f8159051d252bf446130760cd10da256108e59ce. It asserts that each non-Save target exists and invokes the current handler directly. Both enabled-Save mouse activations remain.

The older keyboard-order, disabled-Save, opener-message, detached-render, and lifecycle-coverage repairs are already present and were preserved. No indexed repair supplies this distinct cleanup change. The actual diff follows the prepared evidence record without departures. No production files, accessibility checks, or shared rules changed.

Validation:
Passed node --check and git diff --check. Passed ESLint 9.39.1 with caching disabled and zero errors or warnings, using the current checkout configuration and in-tree Mozilla and SDL plugins. Passed Prettier 3.8.5 with the file's resolved configuration.

The source audit confirmed that all 137 assertions present at current HEAD remain unchanged and that this change adds one assertion site. All ten assertion sites in the repaired lifecycle task have non-empty expected-behavior messages containing should. A separate cumulative audit against 36c4c4e8284c901ba4b7d3adab9ad462ed0b6175 confirmed that all 136 original assertions remain unchanged and in order. The cumulative repair adds two assertion sites. The complete Close behavior test remains unchanged.

Attempted browser_calendarEventEditSave.js through the official Mochitest harness with AUTOCLOBBER=1, MOZ_HEADLESS=1, --flavor browser, --subsuite thunderbird, --headless, and --enable-a11y-checks. The harness exited 1 before application launch because psutil.process_iter() raised PermissionError: [Errno 1] Operation not permitted (originated from sysctl() malloc 1/3). The log is obj-repair-validation/browser-cleanup.log.

A separate xpcshell experiment intended to check native dialog cleanup and real store observers exited 134 with an empty log. None of its assertions are counted as executed. The prior completed routing experiment was not repeated.

Final scope checks show only the intended test file modified, nothing staged, and HEAD unchanged at abb035fd8d657c7d526495095e466977f6378500.

Validation limits:
No native browser, accessibility, or cleanup experiment completed successfully. Matching-source runtime validation and regression failure sensitivity remain incomplete. The available build reports SourceStamp=unknown. No build, clobber, or full Try ran. The commlint wrapper was not repeated after its documented setup-write failures; direct lint and formatting checks passed separately.

The evidence assessment remains incomplete and is not claimed as verification. The accepted local helper supports waiting for the open state to clear before continuing, but it does not demonstrate this exact replacement-dialog scenario. The earlier direct-handler repair also retains its documented style-evidence limit. These implementation choices do not establish shared guidance.

Task-created diagnostic files remain under comm/obj-repair-validation and are excluded from the repair files. No visible browser, commits, staging, Git ref changes, or shared-rule changes were used.

